      "math_academy_id": "e-761",
      "problem": "Find the sum $\\, \\dfrac{2}{3} - \\dfrac{(2/3)^2}{2} + \\dfrac{(2/3)^3}{3} + \\cdots +\\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^n}{n}+\\cdots\\,.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that we can rewrite the given sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x) = \\ln(1+x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x) & =x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\frac{2}{3}) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯ \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{(2/3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2/3)^{3}}{3}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2/3)^{n}}{n}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right).$",
